    "lawCode": "Amd §3011, Pub Health L; add §837-s, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5542A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to duties of the\ncommissioner of health; and to amend the executive law, in relation to\nqualifications to serve as an emergency medical technician\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require a prospective emergency medical technician to\nauthorize a background check for sex offense convictions if the\nindividual chooses to proceed with his or her application.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends section 3011 of the Public Health Law by adding a\nnew subdivision 12 which provides that the Commissioner of Health\nshall require any emergency medical technician application of a\nvolunteer ambulance company or proprietary ambulance service to\nsubject him or herself to a background check pursuant to section 837-s\nof the Executive Law.\n\nSection 2: Amends the Executive Law by adding a new section 837-s\nwhich authorizes the chief of the ambulance company to search for sex\noffense conviction records through the sheriffs department of the\ncounty in which the ambulance company is located within. The\nprospective emergency medical technician has the right to appeal the\ninformation contained in the record of conviction.\n\nSection 3: Effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nVolunteer ambulance companies and proprietary ambulance services\nshould have the ability to deny membership to anyone who is a\nregistered sex offender. Emergency medical technicians are usually in\nsituations where children and families are present and vulnerable due\nto a medical emergency. It is also common for ambulance companies to\nparticipate in community and school events in which children are\npresent. This bill would allow the ambulance company to screen\nprospective volunteers and authorizes the ambulance company to\ndetermine whether the applicant shall be eligible to become a member\nof such company.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect one hundred and twenty days after it shall\nhave become a law.\n\n\n",
